WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400
Case Statements, it can allow you to build a conditional logic in your SQL query

2
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:10.840
by evaluating a list of conditions one by one and return a value when the first condition is met.

3
00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:14.539
So now let's understand the syntax of the Case Statements and what this means.

4
00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:21.000
Okay, so now let's see the syntax step by step.

5
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.239
It starts with the keyword case.

6
00:00:23.239 --> 00:00:27.700
This case indicates now we are starting a logic, a conditional logic in SQL.

7
00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:33.139
It's like programming languages as you start with the if-else, so the if is the keyword of a logic.

8
00:00:33.139 --> 00:00:36.840
And the whole logic as well ends with another keyword called end.

9
00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:41.200
So once SQL sees the end, so this is the end of the conditional logic.

10
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.799
So the case is a start and the end is the end.

11
00:00:43.799 --> 00:00:47.500
So now what we're going to have in between is the conditional logics, right?

12
00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:50.500
So the conditional logic starts with the keyword when.

13
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:54.000
Now we are telling SQL we have a condition to be evaluated,

14
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.099
and then we're going to go and specify that conditional logic.

15
00:00:57.099 --> 00:01:01.900
So now we have to tell SQL what can happen if this condition is fulfilled.

16
00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:04.900
So now we have to use another keyword called then.

17
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:09.199
So now we are telling SQL show these results if the condition is true.

18
00:01:09.199 --> 00:01:10.599
So as you can see, it's very simple.

19
00:01:10.599 --> 00:01:12.300
It's like the natural language, right?

20
00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:13.500
It's like in English.

21
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:16.500
When the condition one is met, then show the results.

22
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:17.599
It's very logic, right?

23
00:01:17.599 --> 00:01:22.000
And now, of course, we can go and add a second condition inside our Case Statements.

24
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.400
So we're going to have the same setup.

25
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:27.699
When condition two, if this is true, then show the result number two.

26
00:01:27.699 --> 00:01:31.400
We specify the keyword when, then we have a second condition.

27
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:35.699
And if this condition is true, then we tell SQL to show another result.

28
00:01:35.699 --> 00:01:38.599
And of course, it's very important to understand in the syntax that

29
00:01:38.599 --> 00:01:43.000
SQL is going to go and process the conditions from the top to the bottom.

30
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.500
So the first most important condition should be at the start.

31
00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:49.300
So SQL is going to first check this condition.

32
00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:53.800
If it fails and it's not true, then it's going to go and jump to the second condition.

33
00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:57.400
So the order of the conditions is very important in your logic.

34
00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:01.000
And now, of course, we can go and add multiple conditions depend on the logic

35
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:02.400
using the keyword when.

36
00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:08.000
And now once we are done defining all the conditions, we can go and specify an else keyword.

37
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.300
The else can introduce the default value, and it is optional.

38
00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:12.600
You can go and skip it.

39
00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:18.699
So the value of the else or the default is going to be used only if all the conditions failed.

40
00:02:18.699 --> 00:02:22.399
So that means all our conditions are not true and nothing is fulfilled.

41
00:02:22.399 --> 00:02:25.699
Then SQL is going to go and use the value from the else.

42
00:02:25.699 --> 00:02:30.300
So it is the default value that's going to be used if all conditions are false.

43
00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:33.800
So those are the keywords that you must use inside each case statement.

44
00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:37.100
So we have case, when, then, and end.

45
00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:38.899
Only the else is an optional.

46
00:02:38.899 --> 00:02:41.000
So you can go and use it or skip it.

47
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.899
So this is the main structure and the syntax of each case statement.

48
00:02:48.899 --> 00:02:55.000
Now let's have a very simple example in order to understand how SQL executes the case statements behind the scenes.

49
00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:58.899
All right, let's have this very simple example where we have only one condition.

50
00:02:58.899 --> 00:03:02.199
So as you can see in the syntax, it starts with case and end.

51
00:03:02.199 --> 00:03:06.300
And then we have only one condition, and we are evaluating here the sales.

52
00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:13.100
So the condition says, if the sales is higher than 50, then show as a result the value of high.

53
00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:14.899
So it's very simple, only one condition.

54
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:19.699
And on the right side, we have here a flowchart in order to understand how the logic is executed.

55
00:03:19.699 --> 00:03:24.237
And now what we're going to do, we're going to go and evaluate those four sales through this logic

56
00:03:24.237 --> 00:03:27.153
and see what the output is going to be with the case statement.

57
00:03:27.199 --> 00:03:28.899
So let's do it one by one.

58
00:03:28.899 --> 00:03:31.399
Let's start with the first sales. It is 60.

59
00:03:31.399 --> 00:03:32.800
So here we're going to go and check.

60
00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:36.300
Is 60 higher than 50? Well, yes.

61
00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:40.699
That means this sales is meeting this condition, and we will get true.

62
00:03:40.699 --> 00:03:43.800
And we're going to get in the output the value of high.

63
00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:47.300
So here we're going to get the value high in the output.

64
00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:52.523
So that means the first sales is fulfilling the requirement, the condition, and SQL is going to give

65
00:03:52.523 --> 00:03:54.247
us the value from this condition.

66
00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:58.600
All right, so now SQL is going to go to the next value, and we're going to start evaluating the 30.

67
00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:00.800
Now we're going to ask the same question, the same condition.

68
00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:03.899
Is 30 higher than 50? Well, no.

69
00:04:03.899 --> 00:04:07.500
So that means in the output for this condition, we will get false.

70
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:10.100
So we will take the path of the false.

71
00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:13.899
Now if you take the path of the false, we will not get any value, right?

72
00:04:13.899 --> 00:04:17.200
So that means the output is going to be a null.

73
00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:19.899
So the output for the 30 is null.

74
00:04:19.899 --> 00:04:24.500
And that's because we didn't define in our logic anything about the default option.

75
00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:26.100
So we don't have here an else.

76
00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:28.399
And this is what's going to happen if you don't use else.

77
00:04:28.399 --> 00:04:31.600
You will get a null in the output for the case statement.

78
00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:33.000
So now let's move to the next one.

79
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.600
It's going to be the same thing.

80
00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:39.100
So 15 is smaller than 50, so it's not fulfilling the condition.

81
00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:41.399
And as well, we're going to get a null.

82
00:04:41.399 --> 00:04:45.000
And for the last one, since it's null, we will get as well a null

83
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:46.799
since it will not fulfill the condition.

84
00:04:46.799 --> 00:04:52.500
So now after evaluating all those sales, only the first sales is fulfilling the condition.

85
00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:54.700
And that's why we have only one value, the high.

86
00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:57.700
All right, so now let's keep moving and adding stuff to our case statements.

87
00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:00.100
Now we are adding a second condition.

88
00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:04.700
So it says, after checking the sales whether it's higher than 50 and it fails,

89
00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:07.899
check again the sales whether it's higher than 20.

90
00:05:07.899 --> 00:05:10.399
If yes, then show the value of medium.

91
00:05:10.399 --> 00:05:16.600
So now in our workflow, we are adding a second condition to be checked if the first one is false.

92
00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:21.500
So now let's go and evaluate our sales again and check the output, the first one, the 60.

93
00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:24.200
So as you can see, the 60 is higher than 50.

94
00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:26.600
So we are fulfilling the first requirement.

95
00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:29.000
That's why we will get the value of high.

96
00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:30.700
So it's same like before.

97
00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:33.200
So here we're going to get high in the output.

98
00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:38.482
And now here, very important to understand one thing is that SQL didn't evaluate here in this

99
00:05:38.482 --> 00:05:40.243
scenario, the second condition.

100
00:05:40.299 --> 00:05:43.200
So SQL didn't waste any time by checking the other condition.

101
00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:46.799
It skipped everything once it gets a true from one condition.

102
00:05:46.799 --> 00:05:53.200
So this is exactly how SQL process the case when it's going to check each conditions from top to down.

103
00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:59.200
And once it finds a true, it's going to stop everything immediately and show the value from this condition.

104
00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:02.000
And it will not evaluate any other conditions.

105
00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:04.000
So now SQL is going to go and jump to the next value.

106
00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:05.700
We are at the value of 30.

107
00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:07.500
So let's evaluate the conditions.

108
00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:11.299
Is 30 higher than 50? Well, it's not. So it's false.

109
00:06:11.299 --> 00:06:12.200
So now what can happen?

110
00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:17.799
SQL is going to go and jump to the next condition and start evaluating the second one, whether it's true or false.

111
00:06:17.799 --> 00:06:18.700
So now we're going to check here.

112
00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:21.899
Is 30 higher than 20? Well, yes.

113
00:06:21.899 --> 00:06:26.000
So it's going to be fulfilled and we will get the value of medium.

114
00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:31.200
So SQL is going to stop everything and show in the output for this value, the medium.

115
00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:32.899
So we're going to get medium here.

116
00:06:32.899 --> 00:06:38.000
So in this scenario, we have evaluated both of the conditions that we have in the case statement.

117
00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:41.000
Now SQL is going to go to the third one. We have 15.

118
00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:43.399
Is 15 higher than 50? Well, no.

119
00:06:43.399 --> 00:06:45.899
So we will get to false for the first condition.

120
00:06:45.899 --> 00:06:48.700
Then it's going to go and jump to the second condition and check it.

121
00:06:48.700 --> 00:06:50.700
Is 15 higher than 20?

122
00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:52.100
Well, as well, no.

123
00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:53.399
So now what's going to happen?

124
00:06:53.399 --> 00:06:58.500
The false is going to be activated over here and we will not get any value as a return.

125
00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:02.000
So we will get the value of null in the output.

126
00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:04.000
And now for the last one, we have null.

127
00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:08.100
We will get as well null because it will not fulfill any of those conditions.

128
00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:11.899
And that's because we didn't define an else in the case statement.

129
00:07:11.899 --> 00:07:17.100
So if we define these conditions like this, we will get the category medium for the 30.

130
00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:20.899
And this is how SQL evaluates multiple conditions in the case statement.

131
00:07:20.899 --> 00:07:26.200
All right, now we're going to go to the final form of our case statements and we're going to go and add an else.

132
00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:28.100
So we're going to have a default value.

133
00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:34.799
So we are seeing here if the sales is not higher than 50 or higher than 20, then show a default value as low.

134
00:07:34.799 --> 00:07:39.799
So that means any sale that is equal or smaller than 20, going to get the value of low.

135
00:07:39.799 --> 00:07:46.299
And now very interesting, if you check the workflow over here, you can see that we have now a value for each path.

136
00:07:46.299 --> 00:07:49.700
So for the first condition, we're going to get high, for the second one, medium.

137
00:07:49.700 --> 00:07:53.600
And if nothing is fulfilled, we're going to get always the value of low.

138
00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:57.100
So there is no way in this chart to get any nulls, right?

139
00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:59.399
So let's go and evaluate again our values.

140
00:07:59.399 --> 00:08:00.399
I think you already get it.

141
00:08:00.399 --> 00:08:06.275
The 60 is fulfilling the first requirement and SQL is going to stop everything immediately and just

142
00:08:06.275 --> 00:08:07.640
show the value of high.

143
00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:13.000
So on the right side over here, nothing is going to be evaluated because the first condition is true.

144
00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:16.100
So here in the outwards, we're going to get the value of high.

145
00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:18.899
So nothing changed like the two previous examples.

146
00:08:18.899 --> 00:08:22.000
Now SQL is going to go to the next value. We have the 30.

147
00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:23.600
So we can evaluate the first one.

148
00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:24.899
It's going to be false.

149
00:08:24.899 --> 00:08:28.500
The next one, it's higher than 20. It is true.

150
00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:31.700
And that's why SQL is going to show the value of medium.

151
00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:36.099
And this is as well, we had it in the previous example. So medium.

152
00:08:36.099 --> 00:08:39.599
So now SQL is going to move to the next one and here things are going to get interesting.

153
00:08:39.599 --> 00:08:41.000
So the value of 15.

154
00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:42.500
We're going to evaluate the first condition.

155
00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:44.599
Is it higher than 50? Well, no.

156
00:08:44.599 --> 00:08:46.700
Is it higher than 20? Well, no.

157
00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:50.599
So now we are in a scenario where none of those conditions are true.

158
00:08:50.599 --> 00:08:54.299
So that's why SQL is going to go and execute the else.

159
00:08:54.299 --> 00:08:59.500
So if you check our chart, it's going to be false and we will get the value of low.

160
00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:04.500
So in the output, we will not get this time a null because we have else.

161
00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:06.000
We will get the value of low.

162
00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:08.000
The same thing now for the null.

163
00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:11.700
Null will not fulfill the first condition as well as the second condition.

164
00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:16.000
And that's why we will get as well the value from the else.

165
00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:19.500
So here in the output, we will get as well the value of low.

166
00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:23.099
So now as you can see, if you use an else inside the case statements,

167
00:09:23.099 --> 00:09:26.700
you will make sure that there will be no nulls in the output.

168
00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:30.299
So that you have learned the different options that we have inside the case statements

169
00:09:30.299 --> 00:09:33.799
and how SQL executes the case behind the scenes.

